   "stem": "A 19-year-old man living in a university residence hall has had 10 days of low-grade fever, headache, malaise and a persistent dry cough. Several students on his floor have had similar illnesses. His chest radiograph shows patchy bilateral interstitial infiltrates, a Gram stain of a small sputum sample shows neutrophils but no bacteria, and cold agglutinins are detected in his serum. He has not improved after 5 days of oral amoxicillin. Which characteristic of the most likely causative organism explains the treatment failure?",
   "options": {
    "A": "It lacks a cell wall, the structure whose synthesis amoxicillin inhibits",
    "B": "It produces a beta-lactamase that hydrolyzes amoxicillin",
    "C": "It expresses the low-affinity penicillin-binding protein PBP2a",
    "D": "It has an outer membrane that prevents amoxicillin from reaching its target"
   },
   "answer": "A",
   "explanation": "A gradual, mild pneumonia with dry cough, interstitial infiltrates, no organisms on Gram stain, cold agglutinins and similar cases among close contacts in a young adult points to Mycoplasma pneumoniae. Mycoplasmas have no cell wall. Beta-lactams such as amoxicillin work by binding penicillin-binding proteins and blocking the cross-linking of peptidoglycan during cell wall synthesis, so they have nothing to act on and M. pneumoniae is inherently resistant to them. The missing cell wall is also why the organism is not seen on Gram stain. CDC lists macrolides, tetracyclines such as doxycycline, and fluoroquinolones as the antibiotic classes used to treat M. pneumoniae infection.",
   "why_not": {
    "B": "Beta-lactamase production, for example by Haemophilus influenzae or Moraxella catarrhalis, is a common reason amoxicillin fails in bacterial respiratory infection. Those organisms have cell walls, are visible on Gram stain and do not cause cold agglutinins.",
    "C": "PBP2a, encoded by the mecA gene, is the basis of methicillin resistance in Staphylococcus aureus. Staphylococcal pneumonia is usually severe, often follows influenza, and shows gram-positive cocci in clusters on Gram stain.",
    "D": "An outer membrane that limits drug entry is a feature of gram-negative bacteria such as Pseudomonas aeruginosa. Mycoplasmas are bounded only by a single cell membrane and have no outer membrane."
   },
