   "competency": "4.2",
   "stem": "A 74-year-old man with atrial fibrillation has taken digoxin 0.125 mg orally once daily at 08:00 for 3 months. Today his heart rate is 72 beats/min, he has no nausea, anorexia or visual change, serum potassium is 4.3 mmol/L and serum creatinine is unchanged from baseline. He takes no new medicines. A serum digoxin concentration drawn at 09:30, 90 minutes after this morning's dose, is reported as 2.6 ng/mL. What is the best interpretation?",
   "options": {
    "A": "Digoxin toxicity is confirmed; stop digoxin and give digoxin immune fab now.",
    "B": "The sample was drawn during the distribution phase; redraw it just before the next dose or at least 6 hours after a dose.",
    "C": "His renal clearance of digoxin has fallen, so the daily dose should be halved from tomorrow.",
    "D": "The result is acceptable for rate control in atrial fibrillation, so no follow-up is needed."
   },
   "answer": "B",
   "explanation": "Digoxin follows two-compartment kinetics. After a dose there is a tissue distribution phase of about 6 to 8 hours, during which serum concentrations are high but do not reflect the concentration at the site of action in the heart. Once distribution is complete, serum and tissue concentrations are in equilibrium and the serum level correlates with effect. The FDA-approved labeling therefore advises obtaining serum digoxin concentrations just before the next scheduled dose or at least 6 hours after the last dose, and interpreting any level in the clinical context rather than changing the dose on an isolated value. This sample was taken 90 minutes after the dose, so a high result is expected and cannot be interpreted. With no symptoms of toxicity, a normal heart rate, normal potassium and stable kidney function, the correct step is to obtain a properly timed level before making any dose decision.",
   "why_not": {
    "A": "Digoxin immune fab is reserved for serious toxicity, such as life-threatening arrhythmias, progressive bradyarrhythmias or hyperkalemia from digoxin excess. This patient has no clinical features of toxicity, and a distribution-phase sample does not establish toxicity.",
    "C": "His serum creatinine is unchanged and he has started no new medicines, so there is no evidence that digoxin clearance has fallen. Cutting the dose on a mistimed sample risks undertreatment.",
    "D": "A distribution-phase sample cannot be used to judge whether the steady-state concentration is appropriate in either direction. The level must be obtained at the correct time before any conclusion is drawn."
   },
