   "stem": "A 29-year-old man of Han Chinese ancestry has newly diagnosed focal epilepsy, and carbamazepine is being considered. Genotyping before treatment shows that he carries HLA-B*15:02. He has never taken carbamazepine, oxcarbazepine or phenytoin. Which plan is most appropriate?",
   "options": {
    "A": "Avoid carbamazepine and choose an antiseizure medicine without a known HLA-B*15:02 association, such as levetiracetam.",
    "B": "Start carbamazepine at a low dose and titrate slowly to reduce the risk of a severe skin reaction.",
    "C": "Avoid carbamazepine and use oxcarbazepine, its better tolerated structural analogue.",
    "D": "Start carbamazepine and check liver enzymes and a complete blood count every 2 weeks for 2 months."
   },
   "answer": "A",
   "explanation": "HLA-B*15:02 is strongly associated with carbamazepine-induced Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis (SJS/TEN). The allele is common in several East and Southeast Asian populations, including Han Chinese, which is why the carbamazepine labeling calls for genotyping at-risk patients before starting. The CPIC guideline for HLA genotype and carbamazepine and oxcarbazepine (2017 update) recommends that a carbamazepine-naive carrier should not be given carbamazepine, and that an oxcarbazepine-naive carrier should not be given oxcarbazepine. Phenytoin, and its prodrug fosphenytoin, should also be avoided in a phenytoin-naive carrier unless the benefit clearly outweighs the risk (CPIC 2020 phenytoin guideline; FDA phenytoin labeling), so switching to phenytoin does not remove the risk. Lamotrigine has only limited evidence of association but still calls for caution. A non-aromatic agent such as levetiracetam, a standard option for focal epilepsy, avoids this genetic risk.",
   "why_not": {
    "B": "The SJS/TEN risk signalled by HLA-B*15:02 is an immune-mediated reaction. Slow titration lowers the rash risk with lamotrigine, but it does not make carbamazepine safe for a carrier; CPIC advises not using carbamazepine at all in a carbamazepine-naive carrier.",
    "C": "Oxcarbazepine is also associated with SJS/TEN in HLA-B*15:02 carriers. The CPIC 2017 update advises not using oxcarbazepine in an oxcarbazepine-naive carrier.",
    "D": "Blood count and liver enzyme monitoring detect hematologic and hepatic adverse effects. It neither predicts nor prevents SJS/TEN, which is the risk this genotype signals."
   },
