   "stem": "A 45-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis is currently receiving adalimumab, a tumor necrosis factor (TNF) inhibitor. Which vaccine is contraindicated while she remains on this therapy?",
   "options": {
    "A": "Inactivated influenza vaccine given by injection",
    "B": "Pneumococcal conjugate vaccine",
    "C": "Yellow fever vaccine",
    "D": "Recombinant hepatitis B vaccine"
   },
   "answer": "C",
   "explanation": "Yellow fever vaccine is a live attenuated viral vaccine. Live vaccines can replicate and cause disease in people whose immune response is suppressed or modulated by drugs, and the CDC Yellow Book lists TNF-alpha inhibitors among the therapies that make yellow fever vaccine contraindicated. The IDSA clinical practice guideline for vaccination of the immunocompromised host (2013) likewise advises against live vaccines in patients with high-level immunosuppression, a group that includes those on biologic immune modulators such as TNF inhibitors. Inactivated, subunit, conjugate and recombinant vaccines cannot replicate, so they are safe in immunosuppressed patients, although the immune response may be weaker. The general principle is to give any needed live vaccine before immunosuppressive therapy starts.",
